#!/bin/sh
# script/lint-fleet: the checks of the agent-fleet tooling, apart from the tree's own lint.
#
# Milestone 889 (the tree's gates stop depending on the fleet tooling), the first step of calef's
# Fork 4 ruling on #1976 (2026-10-12 UTC). design/roadmap/0902-tooling-sorted-by-who-needs-it.md
# sorted the tree's tooling into who needs it, and found script/lint running 22 checks of the
# machinery that runs a fleet of agent lanes: the claim, the merge drain and its detectors, the
# architect label and hold, the session identity, the merge watchers and two method-metric
# collectors. A stranger's lint should not depend on any of that, and when the fleet tooling moves
# to its own repository these checks must go with it rather than be deleted or fetched back.
#
# So they live here, moved whole from script/lint with their reasoning beside them. script/lint
# runs none of them and reads none of their files. ci.yml's `lint` job runs this script as its own
# step, so every check still gates every pull request and merge group; `.githooks/pre-push` runs it
# on a checkout that opts in to the fleet (`git config nife.fleet true`, which script/claim sets).
# Rust tidy and rust-lang/triagebot split the same way: the bot's tests live with the bot.
#
# Each check is the one script/lint ran, at the same path and with the same failure. The 23rd, the
# pre-push hook's fleet half, is new with that hook's split. The last four test the trunk watcher,
# helpers/trunk-health.sh, which is fleet tooling like the merge watchers. None needs a
# network, `gh` or a token: every selftest here uses fixtures or a stub `gh`.
#
#     script/lint-fleet           # all 27, about ten seconds
#
# BUGS
#
# - A check added to the fleet tooling has to be added here by hand; nothing finds an orphaned
#   selftest. Before this split the same was true of script/lint.
# - Rules 4 and 4b read the checked-out branch. On a detached HEAD (every CI pull_request run) both
#   skip, as they did inside script/lint.
#
# Name: provisional, minted 2026-10-12 (UTC) by milestone 889 as the proposal named it. `lint`
# for the act, as script/lint; `-fleet` for whose checks they are, the inventory's word for the
# tooling that runs agent lanes. calef has not ratified it.

set -e
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

# The section numbers (4, 4b, 11, 12) are the ones these checks carried in script/lint, kept so
# that older records citing "lint check 4b" still find them.

echo "==> rule 4: a branch that looks like a milestone claim parses as one"
# 4. **The one branch prefix that carries a mechanism, guarded against near-misses.**
#
#    This was a fixed allowlist of eleven prefixes until 2026-08-17, and calef retired it after
#    asking what the taxonomy was for. The answer, checked rather than argued: **nothing consumes
#    it except this check.** A grep across `script/`, `helpers/`, `.github/workflows/` and `xtask/`
#    for any other reader of a branch prefix returns only false positives (`bench/baseline-*.txt`
#    is a directory, `$prefix/bin` is a shell variable). Only `milestone/N-` is read by anything,
#    and 4b below is what reads it.
#
#    **Every observed failure of the allowlist was a false rejection of legitimate work**, four
#    times: `roadmap/` (the repository's SECOND commonest prefix, refused while ~35 merges already
#    on main used it), `gh-readonly-queue/*` (failed every group build's clippy job, so the queue
#    evicted and rebuilt forever), `dependabot/*`, and `claude/*` (a harness names its own branches
#    and a lane cannot rename them from inside). Each was fixed by widening the list after
#    something broke. That is the signature §61 used to drop three lints and milestone 78 used on
#    three assertions: a check that only ever rejects valid work is measuring the wrong thing.
#
#    **What survives, and why it is the branch name rather than a label.** 4b needs to know which
#    milestone a lane claims, and it runs LOCALLY and OFFLINE: the branch comes from
#    `symbolic-ref`, with no network and no GitHub context. A label cannot carry this. It would
#    put a `gh` call and an authenticated network round trip inside a file-only gate, and it would
#    not exist yet, because a lane runs this before `gh pr create`. The status-accuracy sweep
#    decided the same question the same way on 2026-08-16, refusing `git ls-remote` for the
#    `IN-PROGRESS` check because "the remote needs network in a file-only gate". A branch name is
#    also **fixed when the lane is cut**, where a label is editable at any time; for a check whose
#    job is "you claimed 126, so move 126's block", the claim has to be the immutable half.
#
#    So the taxonomy moves to design/naming.md as a convention, and the only thing still refused is
#    a name that LOOKS like a milestone claim and would therefore skip 4b in silence. That is the
#    one real hole the allowlist was plugging: `milestone-126-pgrep`, with a hyphen, does not match
#    4b's `milestone/*` and would sail past the check that exists to catch a status that never
#    moved.
#
#    A detached HEAD (every CI pull_request run, since actions/checkout builds the merge commit)
#    has no branch to judge and is skipped rather than guessed at, as before.
#
#    **The shape rule itself lives in helpers/branch-name-check.sh** (2026-09-22), not here. This
#    check only ever sees a branch that already exists, which means it fires after the worktree is
#    made, after the work is written, sometimes after commits; script/claim runs the identical
#    refusal before any of that, and a shared case statement is what keeps the two answers from
#    drifting apart. See that file for the near-miss reasoning.
branch="$(git -C "$(dirname "$0")/.." symbolic-ref --quiet --short HEAD || true)"
. "$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/branch-name-check.sh"
nife_check_branch_name_shape "$branch" lint || exit 1

# 4b. **A `milestone/N-*` branch must touch that milestone's roadmap file** (§90, 2026-08-16).
#     The status field lagged the tree four times in one week: 43, 65 and 107 were BUILT for
#     eleven days while their rows said otherwise, and 54 sat hidden behind them. Merging is what
#     finishes a lane, so the status flip belongs in the merge rather than with whoever happens to
#     notice afterwards.
#
#     **The lane makes the edit, and this is the one exception to "a developer never edits
#     design/"** (calef, 2026-08-23; reaffirmed and narrowed 2026-09-01, and now stated in
#     AGENTS.md's developer role rather than only here). The original intent was the reverse: the
#     lane would report the status and the integrator would land the flip separately. That was
#     dropped because the check cannot tell who made an edit anyway, and because a status flip in
#     a different merge from the work is the drift this check exists to catch.
#
#     **Why it was reaffirmed.** On 2026-09-01 two lanes hit this check and both reported it as an
#     impossible instruction, because the failure message below still quoted the retired rule while
#     AGENTS.md's developer bullet forbade the fix. The exception had been real for eight days and
#     lived only in this comment, which no failing lane ever reads. It is narrow: a lane's own
#     milestone's roadmap block and nothing else under design/, not design/decisions/ and not
#     another milestone's block. The drift to watch for is between a lane's claim and the merged
#     tree, and review catches that rather than authorship.
#
#     A branch that leaves the status unmoved still touches the file, if only to say why. That
#     sentence was missing four times.
#
#     **A renumbered lane keeps its branch** (2026-10-10 UTC, #1901): renaming an open pull
#     request's head branch closes the pull request, so the branch keeps the number it was cut
#     with and the check finds the block the lane moved to. The logic and that reasoning live in
#     helpers/milestone-branch-block.sh, so its selftest can plant a renumbered lane and a real
#     mismatch; the selftest runs first, for the reason `fatal-risks --selftest` does.
"$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/milestone-branch-block-selftest.sh" >/dev/null
"$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/milestone-branch-block.sh" "$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)" "$branch" || exit 1

# 10b. **The architect's review packet builds.** helpers/crate-review-packet-selftest.sh builds one
#     packet without a compiler and checks its sections.
"$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/crate-review-packet-selftest.sh" >/dev/null

# 11. **The merge queue's admission predicate says what it means** (2026-09-24 security audit). Until
#     that audit "eligible" admitted a pull request from any fork on GitHub, at a time when the
#     drain armed every eligible pull request as `nife-smelter[bot]`. The predicate is one file,
#     helpers/queue-eligible.jq, and this self-test checks it against fixtures and checks that both
#     consumers still splice it.
echo "==> the merge queue's admission predicate"
"$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/queue-eligible-selftest.sh"
#     And the cancelled-duplicate detection (2026-09-24): the drain reruns, once, the CI run a
#     concurrency group cancelled beside its same-second sibling; the self-test's fixtures are the
#     ordinary supersede that must not be rerun and the attempt-2 run that must not be rerun again.
"$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/cancelled-duplicate-selftest.sh"
#     And the needs-maintainer detector (milestone 727 (a queue eviction goes to a maintainer session), provisional): an ejected, conflicting,
#     stale or unarmed pull request is labelled for a maintainer session and cleared when its cause
#     goes, fed a recorded GraphQL response and one fixture per case, no `gh`. It also fails if the
#     drain arms or enqueues again, which calef ruled out on #1564.
"$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/needs-maintainer-selftest.sh"
#     And the failing-CI flag (provisional name, with helpers/ci-failing.sh): a ready pull request
#     whose newest run of a required check failed is labelled and commented once per head, and a
#     failure a later run superseded is not reported. A recorded commit and synthetic cases, no `gh`.
"$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/ci-failing-selftest.sh"
#     And the same dash `echo` hazard in script/ci-log-baseline (2026-10-31, after issue 2960): CI log
#     text keeps its backslashes through the attribution line. Run under /bin/sh, no `gh`.
"$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/ci-log-baseline-selftest.sh"
#     And who took a dequeue, for milestone 642 (the record should say whether a person or the
#     machinery took a step): helpers/session_steps.py credits a `calef`-account event to the
#     session whose `gh` call came before it, never after. Fixtures only, no `gh`.
python3 "$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/session_steps.py" --selftest
#     And the session identity (milestone 128 (the automation gets its own identity); helper name
#     provisional): helpers/smelter-token signs a JWT with a throwaway key, a local stub of the
#     GitHub API verifies it, and the cache, both permission scopes, git's credential protocol, the
#     `gh` shim's refusal to fall back to a personal login, and the key never reaching output are
#     each checked. No network, and it never reads a keychain.
python3 "$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/smelter-token" --selftest >/dev/null
#     And `Blocked-by: #N` (2026-09-27): the parser (helpers/blocked-by.sh, one number or several
#     on the line) and the draft-unblock decision it feeds (helpers/blocked-by-resolution.jq:
#     unblocked, still waiting, closed unmerged, already labelled) are each fixtures-only, no `gh`.
"$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/blocked-by-selftest.sh"
"$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/blocked-by-resolution-selftest.sh"
#     And the classified claim (calef's ruling R1 on #1894, 2026-10-10 UTC): script/claim refuses a
#     lane with neither or both of `--debt-paydown` and `--new-work`, before git, `gh` or the disk.
#     Both tools are stubs here, so a refusal that regresses fails this rather than claiming.
"$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/claim-selftest.sh" >/dev/null
#     And the decisions renumber, milestone 499 (a renumber that cannot cite the wrong section),
#     provisional name: the collision shift of §194 (sessions interleave rather than serialize),
#     planted in a fixture tree, renaming descending
#     and listing what no filename disambiguates instead of rewriting it.
"$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/decisions-renumber-selftest.sh" >/dev/null
#     And the hourly build-output prune (helpers/prune_build_output.py, name provisional): which
#     worktrees' `target/` may go, including a `wip/` worker judged by its parent lane. Fixture
#     directories and stubbed git, so it reads no real worktree and asks GitHub nothing.
python3 "$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/prune_build_output.py" --selftest >/dev/null
#     And the merged-worktree prune beside it (helpers/prune_worktrees.py, name provisional;
#     milestone 118 (CLAUDE.md has a budget)'s budget rule 2): a worktree goes only when merged, clean, pushed and idle.
#     Throwaway repositories and a stubbed GitHub answer.
python3 "$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/prune_worktrees.py" --selftest >/dev/null
#     Milestone 118's budget rule 3 (both names provisional): `git stash` fails once the block is in,
#     in every worktree, and a push that rewrites a pushed commit is refused. Throwaway repositories.
"$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/stash-block.sh" --selftest >/dev/null
"$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/push-rewrite-check.sh" --selftest >/dev/null

#     The queued-branch push refusal (provisional milestone name: refuse a push to a queued branch;
#     helpers/push-queued-check.sh, called from .githooks/pre-push): queued refuses, not queued
#     passes, gh failing warns and passes, against a fake `gh` on PATH.
"$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/push-queued-selftest.sh" >/dev/null

# 12. The CI-poll refusal hook (helpers/hooks/refuse-ci-polling, wired as a PreToolUse hook on Bash
#     in .claude/settings.json). calef's 2026-09-26 ruling is that a lane never polls CI; this
#     self-test is fixtures only, no stdin and no tree, so it costs nothing to run on every lint.
#     The pre-push hook's fleet half (milestone 889, provisional; helpers/pre-push-fleet.sh), on a
#     checkout with `nife.fleet` set: it reaches `gh`, a failing script/lint-fleet refuses the push,
#     and without the opt-in neither happens. A throwaway repository and a stub `gh`.
echo "==> the pre-push hook's fleet half"
"$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/pre-push-fleet-selftest.sh" >/dev/null

echo "==> the CI-poll refusal hook"
python3 "$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/hooks/refuse-ci-polling" --selftest

echo "==> the agent instructions agree in all five places"
# notes/skills/<name>/SKILL.md, its notes/skills/README.md row, its AGENTS.md link, and its
# .claude/skills and .agents/skills symlinks. helpers/agents-index-check.py's header says why.
python3 "$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/agents-index-check.py"

# §88 (`needs-architect` as a required check, rather than as a script's restraint)'s diff-reading
# half (.github/workflows/architect-label.yml, the general case of
# coe-architect-label.yml): whether a pull request's diff moved the syscall surface, the dependency
# graph, a format crate's wire layout, the spawn protocol, or CLAUDE.md/design/decisions/. The rule
# engine is pure text-in-text-out (helpers/architect-label-rules.py), so its selftest is fixtures
# only, no git and no subprocess, and costs nothing here.
echo "==> the architect-label diff rules"
python3 helpers/architect-label-rules.py --selftest >/dev/null

# `architect-ruled` is read live, not from the event payload (#1712 race, 2026-10-05); stub-gh fixtures.
echo "==> the live architect-ruled read"
helpers/architect-ruled-selftest.sh >/dev/null

# A ruling is posted and the labels swap in one step (lane/architect-queue, 2026-10-06): the marker
# helpers/open-question.jq reads, comment before labels, and --partial leaving the hold; stub-gh.
echo "==> record-ruling posts the ruling and swaps the labels"
helpers/record-ruling-selftest.sh >/dev/null

# A released hold reruns the old red hold runs on the head (#1766, 2026-10-06); stub-gh fixtures plus
# a structural check that architect-ruled-clears-hold.yml listens for the release events.
echo "==> the hold-release rerun"
helpers/hold-rerun-selftest.sh >/dev/null

echo "==> the merge watchers and the method-metric collectors"
# Milestone 723 (a stopped merge watcher is reported within three of its own intervals): the cron to
# interval derivation, the silence rule and its measured floor, and the issue plan (one issue per
# stop, closed on recovery), against fixtures with no `gh` and no network.
python3 helpers/watcher_watch.py --selftest >/dev/null
# Milestone 724 (the merge queue reports its ejection share and its time to merge): the daily
# aggregation, the share, the report's threshold and the CSV upsert, against fixtures with no
# network. The fetch itself runs only in the fleet-metrics and trunk-health workflows.
python3 helpers/merge_queue_share.py --selftest >/dev/null
# The debt-paydown share (lane maintainer/debt-paydown-share-chart, 2026-10-11 UTC): the filter to
# pull requests merged into main and the label test, against fixtures, with no network.
python3 helpers/debt_paydown_share.py --selftest >/dev/null

echo "==> the trunk watcher's cadence stream (a pass that could not ask is a row, never recovery)"
# Fakes and a scenario table in a scratch tree: no network, no gh, no cargo. The defect it holds is
# a recovery line printed about a watcher that could not ask, which a green run of everything else
# would not catch, because it needs the API to fail on cue.
helpers/trunk-health-cadence-selftest.sh >/dev/null

# The repeat half of milestone 422 (a cadence job whose healthy state is red) in
# helpers/trunk-health.sh: the grouping thresholds, the log-frame strip and the watcher exclusions,
# proved offline through a real `--once` pass against a stub `gh` and `git` and synthetic runs (no
# network). helpers/trunk-health-repeat-selftest.sh says what each fixture pins and ends in the
# ruled issue home, helpers/repeat_issue.py, whose pure parsing and planning have their own
# --selftest beside it.
helpers/trunk-health-repeat-selftest.sh >/dev/null
# The DUE half (milestone 495 (a cadence that says "due" reaches nobody)): exit code is the authority, drift is cannot-ask.
helpers/trunk-health-due-selftest.sh >/dev/null
python3 helpers/repeat_issue.py --selftest >/dev/null

echo "lint-fleet: all 27 checks of the fleet tooling passed"
